insight:
If you are working with pure Javascript (no backend framework), you will need to parse the query string from the current URL using the location object:



  const urlParams = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);


Then retrieve the individual values by name, like so:



  const name = urlParams.get('name');
  const email = urlParams.get('email');
  const phone = urlParams.get('phone');


Note that this method will return null if the specified parameter does not exist in the URL.  So to ensure that the parameters are always present, you may want to add some additional validation or handling code to catch any errors.   Also note that this technique relies on the presence of an existing URL parameter in the current window's URL. If you don't have access to the original URL, then you will need to construct the parameter string yourself manually, e.g.:



  window.location.href = "?name=John&email=john@gmail.com&phone=123

In order to include those parameters in the redirect URL, you could use something like this:

window.location.href = 'https://example.com/?name=John&email=john@gmail.com&phone=1234567';

This will append the query string parameters at the end of the URL, and you can then retrieve them in PHP or other languages using the $_GET superglobal array. Alternatively, you could consider using URL rewriting or encoding the values instead, but this depends on your specific requirements.
